--!strict
-- The loading card (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md 8.0, GAME_DESIGN 11): Driftling Isle at dusk over the whole screen from the
-- client's first frame until the first picture of the island is on screen, then it fades into it.
--
-- The user asked for it: "the loading screen isnt centered ... can you also make the loading screen more detailed, like
-- the bakery game, with baked in models and animations?" (2026-11-12). Before, a flat sea-blue curtain with the title
-- laid out at the screen's size when it was made (stale on a window still settling, so it sat off centre).
--
-- Who drives it
-- * ReplicatedFirst.Loading.Boot (a LocalScript that runs before the rest of the game has arrived) puts it up with
--   LoadingCard.show() as its first act and removes Roblox's own loading screen.
-- * The client's Main calls show() on its first line too (nothing when Boot has), moves it through its phases and
--   reports the first picture's progress; finish() once the picture is on screen fills the bar, fades the card out
--   (FADE_SEC, in FADE_STEPS steps) and destroys it. Past SAFETY_SEC it goes by itself.
--
-- What it shows (UI px on a pixel canvas: K screen px each, the game's own pixel size: 2 on a phone, 3 at 1080p), laid
-- out from the canvas's size every time it changes (never a size taken once). The request of 2026-11-20: "redo the
-- loading screen. it should be the floating island now" (before, an island in a dusk sea with a boat going past):
-- * painted into one image: a dusk sky in dithered bands with stars, the sun going down into a sea of clouds (five
--   layers of round billows in flat colour, no outline, each lower and lighter than the one behind it, as the game's
--   own sky has them), far islets floating over it as silhouettes, a palm on the bigger ones;
-- * painted into a second image over it, so that it can bob a pixel up and down (BOB_SEC) with everything on it: the
--   floating island in the middle, as the game builds its islands: its grass top in patches with a lit back edge and
--   tufts and flowers, the grass hanging over the lip, a band of soil, then rows of chunky stones narrowing to a ragged
--   rounded point with a few long chunks hanging under the middle (lit from the top left, darker the deeper they are,
--   the right side in shadow), a 1 px outline round all of it a darker step of the colour inside; vines and roots down
--   its face, a stream falling off its front edge into a puff of mist, stones floating beside it;
-- * on it the game's own models, drawn ahead of time (src/first/LoadingArt, generated by tools/preview/loading_art.sh
--   through Pixel/Snapshot): the Tree of Life at the back (its hollow, fruit and marks glowing, then dimmer), palms,
--   a Driftling Hut, a Cozy Burrow, Tiki Torches, Flower Beds and Round Bushes,
--   the campfire flickering and throwing embers, a Conchy cheering by it; a Frondle and a Pebblit wandering up and
--   down the island with a Mossbun and a Clawdle at their heels, a Gullfin hopping along the front; gulls crossing the
--   sky and stars twinkling;
-- * over the sky, centred: "Driftling Isle" in the pixel font's big face (gold, its outline a darker step of the
--   gold), a short status that follows the real phases, a progress bar in a frame of planks, and at the foot a tip on
--   an ink plate. (Every outline here is a darker step of the colour inside it, never flat ink: the request of
--   2026-11-20, "For outlines everywhere, no longer use pure black, but a darker version of suitable neighbouring
--   pixels instead"; 0.45 of the colour plus 0.3 of the ink, 0.3 and 0.3 for words, as in the game.)
-- Sprites are ImageLabels on the decoded atlas (one EditableImage), moved from one Heartbeat at most SCENE_FPS times a
-- second, at whole UI px. Without EditableImages the card is a plain one: the sky, the clouds, the island in two flat
-- blocks, the title and the bar.
--
-- Nothing from ReplicatedStorage at load (Boot runs before it has arrived): the colours are the palette's written out,
-- and the pixel font (Render/PixelFont, PixelFontData) is mapped beside this module.
--
-- API (client only)
--   LoadingCard.show()           puts the card up (once)
--   LoadingCard.phase(name)      "island" (waiting for the island) | "picture" (drawing it)
--   LoadingCard.progress(f)      the first picture's progress, 0..1 (phase "picture")
--   LoadingCard.finish()         the first picture is on screen: fill, fade out, destroy (once); a cover's: fill, then
--                                the bank takes the card away
--   LoadingCard.cover(opts?)     the card again over the game, for an expansion: a bank of cloud drifts up the screen
--                                and leaves it behind ({ title, status, onCovered, onOpened }; false while it is up)
--   LoadingCard.status(text)     a cover's words under the title
--   LoadingCard.state()          for the sims: { up, phase, bar, finished, fading, painted, fallback, actors, ticks, K,
--                                size, title (its rect: { x0, y0, x1, y1 }, UI px), island ({ cx, cy, rx, ry, depth }),
--                                isle (the island image's box: { x0, y0, w, h }), bob (-1, 0, 1), bobMoves, mode
--                                ("join" | "cover"), cover ("closing" | "held" | "opening"), covered, bank (the bank's
--                                top line, canvas px), clip ({ y, h }: the rows the sprites and words show in), status }
--
-- The cover (the request of 2026-11-20: "make a transition for expansion, hiding the game with a loading transition"):
-- the same card, its root see-through at first. A bank of cloud (three layers of round domes in flat colour, no
-- outline, each a step lighter than the one behind it, the front one with shallow domes hanging under it) rises from
-- under the screen to over it in CLOSE_SEC; the painting is written only where the bank has passed, the rows it
-- crossed since the last frame, and the sprites and words show only in the rows the card wholly covers (a clip under
-- the bank). The card stays up HOLD_MIN at least, then the bank rises again over OPEN_SEC and takes the card with it.
-- Without EditableImages the plain card comes and goes behind a straight edge instead.
